          Appropriations Committee Fiscal Summary

                                SB 1 x (Alpert)

Hearing Date:2/23/99            Amended:2/19/99 
                                
Consultant: Karen French        Policy Vote:Education  9-0
____________________________________________________________ 

BILL SUMMARY: 

SB 1X establishes the Public School Performance and  
Accountability Program consisting of a state Academic  
Performance Index (API), an Immediate  
Intervention/Underperforming Schools Program (IIUSP), and a  
Governor's High Achieving/Improving Schools Program  
(GHAISP).  
 The API ranks all schools in the state based on test  
  results, attendance rates, and graduation rates.  By July  
  1999, the Superintendent of Public Instruction and the  
  State Board of Education must identify growth targets for  
  all schools based on their API baseline score as measured  
  in July 1999, with a minimum percentage growth target of  
  5%.  In addition, the bill requires adoption of a  
  statewide API performance target representing the  
  proficiency level required to meet state academic  
  standards. 
  A school which either meets its own growth target or  
  meets the statewide standard is eligible for a Governor's  
  Performance Award (either monetary or non-monetary) under  
  the GHAISP established by the bill.  Monetary awards are  
  on a per pupil basis, not to exceed $150.  
 The IIUSP provides planning grants in 1999-2000 for 430  
  schools selected from those scoring below  50% on the API  
  and, upon approval of a school's action plan, grants of  
  $150 per enrolled pupil beginning in 2000-01.   At the  
  end of the first year, the local school board could  
  impose serious specified consequences on a school failing  
  to meet its short-term growth.  The second year of  
  implementation (2001-02) provides rewards for a school  
  that meets or exceeds its growth target each year for two  
  years.  A school that has not met its goals, but  
  demonstrates significant growth is eligible for an  
  additional year of funding.  A school that fails to show  
  significant growth will be deemed a deficient school and  
  will be taken over by the SPI. 











